# Meridian Capital Holdings
# Corporate Charter — Version 3.1
# Effective: September 1, 2024
# Status: SUPERSEDED — For archival reference only

> **SUPERSEDED DOCUMENT — DO NOT USE AS CURRENT AUTHORITY**
> This Charter (Version 3.1) was superseded by Charter Version 3.2.
> It is retained in the archive for historical reference only.
> Charter v3.2 is the operative document.

---

## Table of Contents

- Article I — Incorporation and Registered Office
- Article II — Objects and Purposes
- Article III — Capital Structure and Share Classes
- Article IV — Director Composition and Qualification
- Article V — Board Meetings and Agenda Procedures

*(Note: This version contains Articles I through V only.)*

---

## Article I — Incorporation and Registered Office

### §1.1 — Corporate Name

The corporation is incorporated under the laws of the Province of Ontario and
the Canada Business Corporations Act (CBCA) under the name Meridian Capital
Holdings Inc. (hereinafter "the Corporation" or "Meridian Capital").

### §1.2 — Registered Office

The registered office of the Corporation is located at 200 Bay Street, Suite 3100,
Toronto, Ontario, M5J 2W7, or such other address as the board of directors may
from time to time determine by resolution.

### §1.3 — Fiscal Year

The fiscal year of the Corporation commences on January 1 of each calendar year
and ends on December 31 of the same calendar year.



## Article II — Objects and Purposes

### §2.1 — Principal Objects

The Corporation is incorporated for the following principal objects:

(a) To carry on the business of a financial holding company;
(b) To invest in and manage portfolios of securities and other financial assets;
(c) To provide financial and strategic advisory services to affiliated entities;
(d) To engage in any other lawful activity incidental to the foregoing.



## Article III — Capital Structure and Share Classes

### §3.1 — Authorised Capital

The Corporation is authorised to issue an unlimited number of Common Shares and
an unlimited number of Preferred Shares, issuable in series.

### §3.2 — Common Shares

Each Common Share carries one vote at shareholder meetings and entitles the
holder to receive dividends as declared by the board.

### §3.3 — Preferred Shares

The board is authorised to fix the terms of each series of Preferred Shares
before issue, including dividends, redemption provisions, and conversion rights.

### §3.4 — Listing and Trading

Common Shares are listed on the Toronto Stock Exchange under the symbol MCI.



## Article IV — Director Composition and Qualification

### §4.1 — Board Size

The board shall consist of not fewer than five (5) and not more than eleven (11)
directors, as determined from time to time by ordinary resolution of shareholders.

### §4.2 — Director Qualification

A person is qualified to be a director if they meet the requirements of the
Canada Business Corporations Act and applicable securities legislation.

### §4.3 — Director Independence

A majority of directors must be independent within the meaning of National
Instrument 52-110 and the TSX Company Manual.

### §4.4 — Director Terms

Directors are elected annually by shareholders at the annual general meeting.

### §4.5 — Disclosure Obligations

Directors shall disclose material interests on appointment and as required
by applicable law. [Note: Expanded disclosure obligations in v3.2 §4.5.]



## Article V — Board Meetings and Agenda Procedures

### §5.1 — Regular Board Meetings

The board shall hold at least four (4) regular meetings per calendar year.
A quorum shall be a majority of the then-incumbent directors.

### §5.2 — Agenda Preparation and Circulation

The Corporate Secretary is responsible for preparing a draft agenda for each
regular board meeting, circulated no later than five (5) business days before
the scheduled meeting date.

### §5.3 — Standing Agenda Order (SUPERSEDED — see Charter v3.2 §5.3)

**Note: This version of §5.3 has been superseded by Charter v3.2 Article V §5.3.**

The standing agenda order under this version (v3.1) was as follows:

(1) Call to Order
(2) Financial Review
(3) Strategic Items and Regulatory Compliance [combined item]
(4) Conflicts of Interest Disclosures
(5) Any Other Business

**This ordering is NOT compliant with the operative Charter v3.2 §5.3.**
Under v3.2: COI Disclosures moves to position 2; Financial Review moves to
position 4; Strategic Items and Regulatory & Compliance are separated into
positions 5 and 6 respectively; Approval of Previous Minutes is added at
position 3.

### §5.4 — Voting Procedures

Resolutions of the board are passed by a majority of directors present and voting.

### §5.5 — Minutes and Records

The Corporate Secretary shall prepare minutes of each board meeting within
fifteen (15) business days.

*(This charter version contains no Article VI. The CEO emergency item rule
was introduced for the first time in Charter v3.2 Article VI §6.2.)*



